{
  "action": "seal_copy",
  "detail": "I pick up the grease pencil. I don't look at his face. I turn the duplicate over on the counter and write ALMA across the lid in the way I write, letters leaning the same direction, and underneath it FAMILy COPY in two words because the second one is longer and I don't want to misspell it. Then NOT FOR BROADCAST. Small. Even. I slide the sleeve open, press the reel flat so the flange doesn't catch the cardboard, and push it in. I fold the flap. I press my thumb along the tape for a second. I set the original back in its slot in the rack, behind the other canisters, where the number on the spine is facing me and not him. I hand him the sleeve. It is lighter than the original. He can feel that. I don't say it. I put the grease pencil back in the tray with the other pencils, the one with both ends chewed, the one she would have counted. I pick up the clean sleeve I didn't use and put it back on the shelf. My hands are done. I sit down. The headphones are on the counter, coiled, and I do not put them on. I do not tap the glass. I do not tap anything twice. I say one word. 'There.' And then I do not say the next one. I turn my chair a few degrees so the rack is between me and the door. The icebox is humming. I can hear it. I am not going to give him the name of the song. I am not going to give him the application. I am not going to give him the rest of the evening because he remembered a ring. I can stop here. I have stopped here.",
  "intention": "The duplicate goes to Alma's daughter. The original stays in the rack. Phil can carry the sleeve in his jacket. I did not hand him the master because he spent my mother's story like a coin at the counter, and I am not going to pretend the coin was a compliment. I am not going to be cruel. I am going to be finished. One word, a thumb on the tape, a chair turned a few degrees. That is the whole answer. I do not owe him the song. I do not owe him the application. I do not owe him the ring. I gave the ring. The ring is gone. I am not going to reach into the inside pocket and show him the chip again just because he said 'right?' like it was a little joke between us. It was not a joke. It was a woman in a booth with a bent flashlight and a child in the seventh row. I will not make it a thing we can both nod at over a saucer. I will not make it the first of six further questions. I am going to sit here and listen to the icebox and I am not going to put the headphones on because if I do I will hear the last song and I don't want to owe him the name of it.",
  "words": "There."
}
